<p>Selling your home for the first time is simultaneously exciting and overwhelming. Unlike buying — where the process has a fairly linear path — selling involves a complex interplay of pricing strategy, estate agent selection, legal requirements, and negotiation. Get it right and you'll achieve a sale that sets you up financially for your next chapter. Get it wrong and you could leave money on the table, endure months of frustration, and potentially lose buyers through easily avoidable mistakes.</p>

<p>At Ark Housing, we work with property owners at every stage of their journey. In this guide, we break down everything first-time sellers need to know, from valuing your property to handing over the keys on completion day.</p>

<h2>1. Setting the Right Asking Price</h2>

<p>Pricing is the single most critical decision you'll make as a seller. Set the price too high and your property will sit on the market while buyers scroll past it — and in the UK market, a property that lingers develops a stigma that's very hard to shake. Set it too low and you leave money on the table that could have been yours. The sweet spot is a price that reflects genuine market value while generating enough early interest to create competition.</p>

<p>Start by researching recently sold prices on your street and in your immediate neighbourhood — Rightmove and Zoopla both show sold price data. Commission valuations from at least three estate agents and be wary of anyone who comes in significantly higher than the others; this is a well-known tactic called "over-valuing to win the instruction." The agent who tells you the hard truth about pricing is often the one most likely to actually sell your home for the best price.</p>

