<h2>2. Choosing the Right Estate Agent</h2>

<p>Not all estate agents are created equal. You're looking for an agent with genuine local market knowledge, a strong track record of actual sales (not just listings), a proactive attitude, and clear, honest communication. Ask each agent you interview how many properties they've sold in your area in the last six months, what their average percentage of asking price achieved is, and how they plan to market your property specifically.</p>

<p>Consider the quality of their photography — poor listing photos are one of the fastest ways to lose potential buyers online. The vast majority of buyers now start their search on Rightmove or Zoopla, so the quality of your online listing is paramount. Ask to see examples of listings they've created for similar properties. Check their reviews on Google and Trustpilot. Most importantly, choose someone you trust and feel comfortable communicating with, as you'll likely be in regular contact for weeks or months.</p>

<h2>3. Preparing Your Home for Sale</h2>

<p>Before the photographer arrives and the first viewer crosses the threshold, your home needs to be in the best possible condition. This doesn't mean a costly renovation — buyers generally adjust their offers to reflect cosmetic work they plan to do themselves. But it does mean resolving anything that's obviously broken, making the property feel clean and well-maintained, and presenting it as attractively as possible within your budget.</p>

<p>Fix dripping taps, sticking doors, broken light switches, and cracked tiles. These small imperfections send a signal to buyers that the property hasn't been cared for, and they'll start wondering what bigger problems might be lurking. A deep clean, a fresh coat of paint in neutral colours, and thoughtful decluttering will do more for your sale than any expensive improvement. See our full guide to home staging for detailed tips on presentation.</p>

<h2>4. Understanding the Legal Process</h2>

<p>As a seller, you'll need to instruct a solicitor or licensed conveyancer to handle the legal side of your sale. This process — conveyancing — involves drafting the contract, responding to buyer enquiries, dealing with searches, and ultimately facilitating the transfer of title. Costs typically range from £1,000 to £2,000 plus disbursements.</p>

<p>You'll be required to complete a Property Information Form (TA6) and a Fittings and Contents Form (TA10), both of which disclose important information about the property to the buyer. Answer these honestly and thoroughly — failing to disclose known issues can expose you to legal action after completion. You'll also need an Energy Performance Certificate (EPC) before you can market the property; if you don't have a valid one, your estate agent can usually arrange this for you.</p>

